issues.body:
**Simple scenario**:
A bank manager asks an AI assistant to approve a loan. The AI asks a credit-check tool to verify the applicant credit risk analysis and similarly delegates one more task to another agent to get the credit score for verification. The spawned credit-agent needs to know: did a real bank manager ultimately authorize this, or did some random agent trigger it on its own to spawn another agent to fetch credit score.?

When Agent A delegates a task to Agent B, that delegation is an act of authority transfer — not just a function call. actchain describes who is calling. delegation_chain term describes who authorized whom to call whom.

**Why delegation_chain, not actchain**
1.  actchain also carries the same meaning, but somewhere not resonating with delegation as core concept.
2. delegation_chain is a specific term describes who authorized whom to call whom.
3. delegation_depth as a companion integer allows any service to enforce max-hop policies without traversing the nested chain.

**Proposed claim**
"delegation_chain": {
  "act": {
    "sub": "credit-check-tool",
    "act": {
      "sub": "ai-assistant",
      "act": {
        "sub": "bank-manager@bank.com"
      }
    }
  }
}
"delegation_depth": 3

comments.body:
@JayDaley – flagging prior art is fair behaviour, we were doing this in good faith.

For the avoidance of doubt, Gimel Foundation does not grant any IP rights to IETF at this point. Our IP rights are constrained, limited and subject to Exclusions, based on Gimel Foundation licensing, Legal Terms, with patents pending. Gimel Foundation’s GitHub page (https://github.com/Gimel-Foundation) is providing the canonical licensing and legal terms. 

Gimel Foundation would be open to discuss questions and licensing under the Gimel framework that works for both sides.

@GoetzGW  There is no concept of granting patent rights/licensing to the IETF.  When someone contributes to the IETF, that does not include patent rights.  Similarly the IETF does not hold any patents and therefore there is no concept of the IETF licensing its standards.

If you are indeed acting in good faith then please respect our processes and use our IPR Disclosure form.  This is the way professional SDOs work - through mature and developed processes, not random issues in a GitHub repository.

The purpose of an IPR disclosure is for you to officially put on the record your intellectual property claim and the potential relevance to IETF work.  In doing that you can commit to licensing (for third party implementers not the IETF) but you are not required to.  Please at least read the relevant IPR Disclosure [form](https://datatracker.ietf.org/ipr/new-specific/) to understand what it involves.
